34:["$","h2","h2-2",{"id":"dhul-hulayfah-abyar-ali-the-madinah-miqat","children":"Dhul Hulayfah (Abyar Ali) — the Madinah Miqat"}]
35:["$","p","p-3",{"children":["Dhul Hulayfah is the Miqat for anyone travelling ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"from Madinah to Makkah"}],", and it is the farthest Miqat from Makkah. The mosque sits on the southern edge of Madinah, a short drive from the central area around Masjid an-Nabawi, and has large washing and changing facilities built for pilgrims."]}]
36:["$","p","p-4",{"children":["In practice this is the Miqat most international pilgrims meet by road, because many itineraries are \"Madinah first, then Makkah\". If you go by private car, you change at your hotel or at the mosque, pray there, make your intention, and continue south on the Hijrah road. The full ",["$","$L26","a-0",{"href":"/routes/madinah-to-makkah","children":"Madinah to Makkah transfer"}]," is about 430 km and roughly 4 hours of driving, plus whatever time you spend at the Miqat."]}]
37:["$","p","p-5",{"children":"If you take the Haramain train from Madinah instead, you need to be in Ihram before the train passes the Miqat, so most pilgrims change at their hotel or stop at Abyar Ali on the way to Madinah station."}]
38:["$","h2","h2-3",{"id":"al-juhfah-near-rabigh","children":"Al-Juhfah — near Rabigh"}]
39:["$","p","p-6",{"children":"Al-Juhfah, close to the town of Rabigh north of Jeddah, is the Miqat for the north-western approach — historically for pilgrims from the Levant, Egypt and North Africa, and today for anyone driving south along the Red Sea coast road. It is also where some pilgrims go if they arrive in Jeddah without Ihram (see below), which makes it a common reason for a \"there and back\" trip from Jeddah."}]
3a:["$","h2","h2-4",{"id":"qarn-al-manazil-as-sayl-al-kabir-and-wadi-muhrim","children":"Qarn al-Manazil — As-Sayl al-Kabir and Wadi Muhrim"}]
3b:["$","p","p-7",{"children":["Qarn al-Manazil is the Miqat for travellers coming from ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Taif, Riyadh and the east"}]," by road. There are two mosques associated with it, one on each road down to Makkah:"]}]
3c:["$","ul","ul-0",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"As-Sayl al-Kabir"}]," on the As-Sayl road, the route most traffic from Riyadh and the GCC uses."]}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Wadi Muhrim"}]," on the Al-Hada mountain road from Taif."]}],"\n"]}]
3d:["$","p","p-8",{"children":["If you are travelling ",["$","$L26","a-0",{"href":"/routes/riyadh-to-makkah","children":"Riyadh to Makkah by private car"}]," — about 870 km and around 8 hours — the Miqat comes near the end of a very long drive, so it makes sense to plan your change of clothes and the stop before you set off. From Taif, the Miqat is only a short way into the journey down the escarpment."]}]
3e:["$","h2","h2-5",{"id":"yalamlam-and-dhat-irq","children":"Yalamlam and Dhat Irq"}]
3f:["$","p","p-9",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Yalamlam"}]," (As-Sa'diyyah) serves the southern approach along the coast, including pilgrims coming from Yemen and the south of the Kingdom. ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"Dhat Irq"}]," serves the north-eastern approach. Few international visitors meet either by road, but both matter if you are driving from those directions."]}]
40:["$","h2","h2-6",{"id":"flying-into-jeddah-ihram-on-the-plane","children":"Flying into Jeddah: Ihram on the plane"}]
41:["$","p","p-10",{"children":["This is the question most first-time pilgrims ask. ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Jeddah lies inside the Miqat boundary"}],", so if you fly to Jeddah intending Umrah, your aircraft will cross the Miqat line before landing. You therefore need to be in Ihram — or at least make your intention — before that point."]}]
42:["$","ul","ul-1",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":"Most airlines serving Jeddah announce the Miqat before the aircraft reaches it. Change into your Ihram garments before boarding or during the flight, and make your intention when the announcement is made."}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":"Many pilgrims change at their departure airport, because aircraft washrooms are small and busy."}],"\n",["$","li","li-2",{"children":["After landing, you go straight from ",["$","$L26","a-0",{"href":"/routes/jeddah-airport-to-makkah","children":"Jeddah Airport to Makkah"}]," — about 80 km, roughly an hour by car — already in Ihram."]}],"\n"]}]
43:["$","h3","h3-0",{"id":"if-you-land-in-jeddah-without-ihram","children":"If you land in Jeddah without Ihram"}]
44:["$","p","p-11",{"children":["Scholars differ on the exact ruling for someone who passed the Miqat by air without Ihram, so ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"ask a scholar you trust before you travel"}],". A common precaution is to travel out to a Miqat — usually Al-Juhfah near Rabigh — enter Ihram there, and then continue to Makkah. That turns a one-hour airport transfer into a much longer journey, so if you know in advance that this will be your plan, book it as one trip with a stop rather than two separate rides."]}]
45:["$","h3","h3-1",{"id":"living-or-staying-in-jeddah","children":"Living or staying in Jeddah"}]
46:["$","p","p-12",{"children":"People who are already residing inside the Miqat boundary — residents of Jeddah, for example — generally enter Ihram from where they are. If you are a visitor who has been in Jeddah on business and then decides to perform Umrah, check the ruling that applies to your situation with a scholar."}]
47:["$","h2","h2-7",{"id":"planning-a-miqat-stop-on-a-private-transfer","children":"Planning a Miqat stop on a private transfer"}]
48:["$","p","p-13",{"children":"A private car is the most flexible way to handle the Miqat because the stop is built into your journey rather than squeezed around a timetable. A few practical points:"}]
49:["$","ul","ul-2",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Say so when you book."}]," Mention that you need a Miqat stop and roughly how long you will need — washing, changing and two rak'ahs usually take 20–40 minutes, longer for families with children."]}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Pack the Ihram where you can reach it."}]," Keep the garments, a small towel, unscented soap and sandals in a hand bag, not in a suitcase at the bottom of the boot."]}],"\n",["$","li","li-2",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Mind the clock."}]," Arriving in Makkah at night, after a long flight and a Miqat stop, is common; choose a departure time that leaves you enough energy for Tawaf and Sa'i."]}],"\n",["$","li","li-3",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Families and older pilgrims"}]," often prefer a ",["$","$L26","a-0",{"href":"/fleet","children":"full-size SUV or van"}]," for the Madinah–Makkah leg, so everyone can change and rest without unloading the luggage."]}],"\n"]}]
4a:["$","h2","h2-8",{"id":"which-miqat-applies-to-my-trip","children":"Which Miqat applies to my trip?"}]
4b:["$","div","table-1",{"className":"blog-table not-prose","children":["$","table",null,{"children":[["$","thead","thead-0",{"children":["$","tr","tr-0",{"children":[["$","th","th-0",{"children":"Your journey"}],["$","th","th-1",{"children":"Miqat you pass"}],["$","th","th-2",{"children":"Notes"}]]}]}],["$","tbody","tbody-0",{"children":[["$","tr","tr-0",{"children":[["$","td","td-0",{"children":"Madinah → Makkah (car or train)"}],["$","td","td-1",{"children":"Dhul Hulayfah (Abyar Ali)"}],["$","td","td-2",{"children":"Change at the hotel or at the mosque"}]]}],["$","tr","tr-1",{"children":[["$","td","td-0",{"children":"Fly into Jeddah → Makkah"}],["$","td","td-1",{"children":"Crossed in the air"}],["$","td","td-2",{"children":"Ihram on the plane, before the announcement point"}]]}],["$","tr","tr-2",{"children":[["$","td","td-0",{"children":"Taif → Makkah"}],["$","td","td-1",{"children":"Qarn al-Manazil (Wadi Muhrim or As-Sayl al-Kabir)"}],["$","td","td-2",{"children":"Depends on which road you take"}]]}],["$","tr","tr-3",{"children":[["$","td","td-0",{"children":"Riyadh or the GCC → Makkah by road"}],["$","td","td-1",{"children":"Qarn al-Manazil (As-Sayl al-Kabir)"}],["$","td","td-2",{"children":"Near the end of a long drive — plan the stop"}]]}],["$","tr","tr-4",{"children":[["$","td","td-0",{"children":"Coastal road from the north"}],["$","td","td-1",{"children":"Al-Juhfah"}],["$","td","td-2",{"children":"Near Rabigh"}]]}]]}]]}]}]
4c:["$","h2","h2-9",{"id":"common-questions","children":"Common questions"}]
4d:["$","h3","h3-2",{"id":"can-i-enter-ihram-at-my-hotel-before-reaching-the-miqat","children":"Can I enter Ihram at my hotel before reaching the Miqat?"}]
4e:["$","p","p-14",{"children":["Yes. You may put on the Ihram garments and even make your intention before you reach the Miqat; what you may not do is ",["$","em","em-0",{"children":"pass"}]," the Miqat without being in Ihram. Many pilgrims leaving Madinah change at their hotel and simply pray at Abyar Ali on the way."]}]
4f:["$","h3","h3-3",{"id":"is-jeddah-a-miqat","children":"Is Jeddah a Miqat?"}]
50:["$","p","p-15",{"children":"No. Jeddah is inside the Miqat boundary. Pilgrims flying in enter Ihram before the aircraft crosses the boundary."}]
51:["$","h3","h3-4",{"id":"do-i-need-ihram-to-go-from-madinah-to-makkah-if-i-am-not-doing-umrah-straight-aw","children":"Do I need Ihram to go from Madinah to Makkah if I am not doing Umrah straight away?"}]
52:["$","p","p-16",{"children":"The obligation applies when you are heading to Makkah intending Umrah or Hajj. If your plans are different, ask a scholar about your specific case."}]
53:["$","h3","h3-5",{"id":"can-the-driver-wait-while-we-change-at-the-miqat","children":"Can the driver wait while we change at the Miqat?"}]
54:["$","p","p-17",{"children":"On a private transfer, yes — the stop is part of the trip you book. Tell us how long you expect to need so the journey is planned around it."}]
